@title Plural Value Accounting
@audience practice-based researchers, social enterprise partners, impact leads
@tone formal-analytic
@style design-pattern
@sigils [⚖️/多 🌍/值]
@see or3/skills-that-travel-onward, or2/bioliteracy-open-value

! conclusion: Plural Value Accounting

  + context: Your project produces value that the available accounting cannot see.

  + IF:
    You work on something whose humanitarian, ecological, and cultural effects are the point, and you are asked to account for it.

  + HOWEVER:
    Single-dimensional accounting wins by default, because price is legible and the other dimensions are not. This holds even among pro-social partners — NGOs and social enterprises reach for cost per beneficiary as readily as anyone — so the dimensions that motivated the work drop out of its own reporting, and consumer or funder decisions are made as if they did not exist.

  + THEN:
    Make the plural accounting an explicit object of the work rather than a caveat about it. Hold financial, humanitarian, and ecological considerations together and show them through lived experience — what a thing cost to make, who made it, under what conditions, at what environmental cost — rather than compressing them into a composite metric. Treat the project as an experimental site for the alternative accounting, and report it as a finding.

  + BECAUSE:
    Compressing plural values into one number reproduces the problem the work exists to expose; keeping them visible and incommensurable is what lets people see the trade-off they are actually making. Framed as a research contribution rather than an excuse, it also gives the work a claim that a cost-per-beneficiary figure never could.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Name the dimensions your work operates on and report each separately, resisting the composite score.]
    next[Look for the point of decision — purchase, commission, funding — and ask what is invisible there.]
